WEBVTT

00:00:00.583 --> 00:00:05.125
These graphs have four vertices. How many
roots of their matching polynomials lie

00:00:05.125 --> 00:00:07.833
above seven fifths, counting multiplicities?

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:13.292
We will design an atlas to answer that
question. Each component must contribute to

00:00:13.292 --> 00:00:15.708
the count, coverage, or diagnosis.

00:00:16.625 --> 00:00:21.500
First try the method on a cubic. Its Lean
proof is small, and it shows how to turn

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.917
input conditions into a uniform guarantee.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:31.833
For this cubic, p_c(t), the input is a real
number c. The output we want is a proof

00:00:31.833 --> 00:00:33.708
of at least three distinct real roots.

00:00:34.625 --> 00:00:39.500
Look at the obligations that card opens.
Supply a nonzero polynomial,

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.458
four strictly increasing points, and a
negative product for each pair of

00:00:43.458 --> 00:00:44.917
neighboring evaluations.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:53.083
Our four witnesses are minus two, minus one,
one and two. Move c and the curve changes,

00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:55.292
while those four sample locations stay put.

00:00:56.167 --> 00:01:02.167
Evaluate exactly. The first value is c minus
two, the second is c plus two,

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:04.583
and those same two values repeat in that
order.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:11.500
To get minus, plus, minus, plus, require c
minus two to be negative and c plus two

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.500
positive. This is equivalent to minus two
less than c less than two.

00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:22.333
Here is the checked theorem. It keeps a real
parameter and takes the two strict bounds.

00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:25.125
Its conclusion asserts at least three
distinct roots.

00:01:26.042 --> 00:01:31.125
Apply the alternating-sign theorem to the
nonzero polynomial and the witness vector.

00:01:31.125 --> 00:01:34.750
Two holes remain, one for the ordering and
another for the signs.

00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:39.958
The card turns those facts into three
disjoint intervals containing roots.

00:01:39.958 --> 00:01:44.208
Lean checks a single declaration for every c
satisfying its assumptions.

00:01:45.125 --> 00:01:50.417
We tried a Newton branch. It checks, but
substituting the coefficients reduces it to

00:01:50.417 --> 00:01:54.667
zero at least minus twenty-seven. We leave
that branch out of the machine.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:01.583
The polynomial and its goal stay the same,
but one evaluation becomes zero.

00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:05.958
The diagnostic can identify the first
product that is no longer negative.

00:02:06.833 --> 00:02:11.208
Now set c to two with the original
witnesses. The product fails for a different

00:02:11.208 --> 00:02:14.333
reason. We have reached the boundary of the
uniform proof.

00:02:15.208 --> 00:02:20.083
At the boundary we have a different proof.
The factorization into t minus one squared

00:02:20.083 --> 00:02:25.000
times t plus two exhibits a double root.
Lean checks the identity by expansion.

00:02:26.792 --> 00:02:31.667
Return to the atlas. The input is a graph
and a rational threshold.

00:02:31.667 --> 00:02:36.333
We want disjoint intervals, multiplicities,
and the number of roots strictly above the

00:02:36.333 --> 00:02:36.875
threshold.

00:02:37.792 --> 00:02:42.250
Work backwards: obtain the coefficients from
the graph, bound the search,

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.542
justify the counts, and prove coverage.

00:02:45.458 --> 00:02:49.625
This actual Excovery query, from a
polynomial to a root count,

00:02:49.625 --> 00:02:52.667
proposes the Budan Fourier bound among other
candidates.

00:02:53.583 --> 00:02:58.292
A bound can rule out intervals. But our
answer also requires an exact count with

00:02:58.292 --> 00:03:01.333
multiplicities. Select a rule by that
guarantee.

00:03:02.208 --> 00:03:07.042
If the bridge from graph to coefficients
remains open, a proof about that polynomial

00:03:07.042 --> 00:03:09.000
does not yet answer the graph question.

00:03:10.792 --> 00:03:15.000
The matching polynomial uses counts of
disjoint edge sets of each size as

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.417
coefficients, with alternating signs.

00:03:18.292 --> 00:03:23.750
The four-vertex path has three edges and one
two-edge matching. Its polynomial is t to

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:26.333
the fourth minus three t squared plus one.

00:03:27.208 --> 00:03:32.167
Two disjoint edges give another polynomial,
t to the fourth minus two t squared plus

00:03:32.167 --> 00:03:32.417
one.

00:03:33.333 --> 00:03:37.958
Heilmann and Lieb's results in cards sixty-
nine and seventy give real roots and a

00:03:37.958 --> 00:03:42.875
degree-based bound. With maximum degree at
most two, start from minus two to two.

00:03:43.792 --> 00:03:49.375
Sturm certifies exact counts for square-free
factors. If an endpoint is itself a root,

00:03:49.375 --> 00:03:51.667
check that zero and record it separately.

00:03:52.542 --> 00:03:57.125
The factors, raised to their multiplicities,
must recover the polynomial.

00:03:57.125 --> 00:04:00.917
With real splitting established, the
weighted counts must sum to its degree.

00:04:01.792 --> 00:04:07.167
Each piece has a concrete job: establish the
input data, decide where to search,

00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:09.083
or prove that the atlas is complete.

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:15.458
Let's check the count for the four-vertex
path. We want roots strictly above seven

00:04:15.458 --> 00:04:21.542
fifths. The global bound is two and the
polynomial at two is five, so we can use two

00:04:21.542 --> 00:04:22.583
as the upper endpoint.

00:04:23.542 --> 00:04:28.583
Sturm works on a finite interval. Card
thirteen needs a square-free factor,

00:04:28.583 --> 00:04:32.250
ordered endpoints, and neither endpoint can
be a root of that factor.

00:04:33.167 --> 00:04:36.458
Start the sequence with the polynomial and
its derivative.

00:04:36.458 --> 00:04:40.208
Each step appends the negative remainder of
the preceding division.

00:04:40.208 --> 00:04:42.542
These are the five polynomials for the path.

00:04:43.458 --> 00:04:49.667
Evaluate the sequence. At seven fifths, a
minus sign is followed by four plus signs.

00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:51.500
At two every sign is positive.

00:04:52.417 --> 00:04:57.833
The variation difference is one minus zero.
That is why the interval contains one root.

00:04:57.833 --> 00:05:01.167
Since the factor occurs once, the weighted
count is also one.

00:05:02.083 --> 00:05:06.667
For two disjoint edges, the factor t squared
minus one occurs twice.

00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:11.083
At a threshold of one, the endpoint is a
root, so the Sturm hypothesis needs

00:05:11.083 --> 00:05:11.625
attention.

00:05:12.542 --> 00:05:17.583
Record one as an exact point of multiplicity
two, exclude it from the strict query,

00:05:17.583 --> 00:05:21.458
and remove its linear factor before counting
the remaining roots.

00:05:21.458 --> 00:05:24.417
The remaining factor no longer vanishes at
the endpoint.

00:05:25.333 --> 00:05:29.958
The prototype computes the coefficients two
ways and uses exact arithmetic for the

00:05:29.958 --> 00:05:34.500
count. This panel shows how a Lean machine
would reproduce and check that count.

00:05:36.292 --> 00:05:40.875
Return to seven fifths. Compare the path and
the two disjoint edges at the same

00:05:40.875 --> 00:05:41.458
threshold.

00:05:42.375 --> 00:05:48.000
For the path, the largest positive root lies
between eight fifths and thirteen eighths.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.958
The other lies between three fifths and five
eighths.

00:05:51.875 --> 00:05:56.000
The negative intervals are reflections. All
four roots are simple,

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.917
and only the largest interval lies above
seven fifths.

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:04.417
In the other graph, the polynomial is t
squared minus one, squared.

00:06:04.417 --> 00:06:07.500
The roots minus one and one each have
multiplicity two.

00:06:08.375 --> 00:06:13.125
At seven fifths the count is zero. If the
threshold is one, the double root at the

00:06:13.125 --> 00:06:15.667
threshold is excluded by strict comparison.

00:06:16.583 --> 00:06:21.167
At three quarters, the double root
contributes two. The same atlas can answer

00:06:21.167 --> 00:06:23.792
the new question without approximating the
roots again.

00:06:24.708 --> 00:06:29.208
These worked examples have exact
calculations. For general inputs,

00:06:29.208 --> 00:06:34.500
those calculations still need to be wired to
Lean checks of origin, factors, and counts.

00:06:36.292 --> 00:06:40.292
Each piece must close an obligation that
affects the answer, coverage,

00:06:40.292 --> 00:06:42.875
or diagnosis. Otherwise leave it out.

00:06:43.792 --> 00:06:48.583
Card sixty-eight transfers the matching
polynomial to the adjacency characteristic

00:06:48.583 --> 00:06:52.917
polynomial for forests. Connect it only if
the query needs that spectrum.

00:06:53.833 --> 00:06:59.042
The cubic prototype already has a Lean
check. The atlas has exact worked examples

00:06:59.042 --> 00:07:00.917
and formal adapters still to build.

00:07:01.792 --> 00:07:06.208
Test the design by accepting the same counts
on a suite of graphs and rejecting an

00:07:06.208 --> 00:07:10.167
altered coefficient, an omitted root, and a
false multiplicity.

00:07:11.083 --> 00:07:15.417
Try a threshold in the simulation and
download the Lean proof for the family.

00:07:15.417 --> 00:07:19.667
A reproducible result and a diagnostic turn
the pieces into a machine.
